### Title
Package version and build ordering in conda search output.

### Why
When sharded repodata is enabled, `conda search` displayed package versions in lexicographic string order rather than `VersionOrder` (e.g., `1.11.5` before `1.3.0`) and sorted build strings lexicographically rather than by numeric build number (e.g. `10` before `2`).

### Platforms
- [ ] Windows
- [ ] macOS
- [ ] Linux

### Prerequisites
Internet access to default channels or access to a channel serving sharded repodata.

### Steps
1. Execute `conda search maturin --override-channels -c defaults`.
2. Inspect the sequence of version numbers displayed in the `Version` column.
3. Compare the relative position of `1.3.0` and `1.11.5`.
4. Inspect packages with multi-digit build numbers (e.g., builds with `_2` and `_10`) and observe relative build order.

### Pass criteria
Versions are sorted according to Conda version semantics (`VersionOrder`): `0.14.17` precedes `1.3.0`, `1.3.0` precedes `1.9.4`, and `1.9.4` precedes `1.11.5`. Single-digit minor versions appear before double-digit minor versions with the same major version. Builds sharing a version are ordered by numeric build number (e.g., build 2 precedes build 10).

### Out of scope / notes
Non-standard PEP 440 prerelease parsing edge cases (covered by #11721).
